  "answer_text": "Great question! It can feel a little awkward at first, but with a bit of understanding and practice, it becomes very natural. Here are some helpful tips:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f How to Make Eye Contact with Someone Who Has a Lazy Eye\n\n**1. Look at the eye that's looking at you**\n\nLook at the eye which is looking at you. The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be, it can feel to them as though you are staring at their lazy eye.\n\n**2. Remember \u2014 they ARE making eye contact with you**\n\nWhat can throw you off is that it looks like one eye is looking at you and the other is looking somewhere else, which can make it feel like they're not paying attention. Keep in mind that both their eyes are in fact looking at you \u2014 one of them just has a hard time staying centered.\n\n**3. Don't pause or act confused \u2014 just carry on**\n\nFor most people with a lazy eye, they feel like they're staring right at you. So don't pause and wonder if their attention is on you \u2014 just carry on with what you're saying.\n\n**4. Try softening or slightly blurring your focus**\n\nBlurring your eyes slightly lets you look straight at them and listen better, without getting distracted by trying to figure out which eye to focus on. This is a practical trick many people find helpful.\n\n**5. Don't stare or make it obvious you're figuring out which eye to look at**\n\nPeople with a lazy eye can tell when someone is trying to figure out which eye to look at \u2014 it can be uncomfortable for them. Most prefer you simply look at the eye that is focused on you.\n\n**6. With close friends or colleagues, it's okay to gently acknowledge it**\n\nIf you can't concentrate, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. Something lighthearted like \"sometimes your eye moves and it throws me off\" can lighten a possibly awkward situation \u2014 but only with someone you know well.\n\n**7. With strangers or acquaintances, just be natural**\n\nWhen you're talking to someone you'll probably never see again, it's not worth making them feel self-conscious. If you feel uneasy, find another point on their face to focus on.\n\n**8. It gets easier over time**\n\nMuch like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them, and other things about that person start to form your dominant image.\n\n---\n\nThe most important takeaway is to be **respectful and natural**. People with a lazy eye are very aware that it can be distracting, so the kindest thing you can do is focus on the engaged eye and treat the conversation as you normally would!",
